[A2(b) THE BRANCHING BOUNDS — W4b with each record's OWN measured B, and the refinement this unit's records force]
  W4b (D63, verified there): if every event carries at most B registers then
  |D_e(d)| <= B^d.  A two-proposer arbitration carries THREE registers
  (proposers u {new version}), so W4b's bound at d = 2 is 9 — and D63's
  residue 1 asked whether a conflict record can use them.
  W4c THE MINT-REGISTER REFINEMENT (this unit).  The version register minted
  by an arbitration is a BIRTH WIRE with NO P-successor.  Replacing |regs(x)| by
  the number of registers of x that RECUR (the live out-degree b(x)) in W4b's
  own proof gives |D_e(d)| <= Bl^d with Bl = max b(x) — and for an arbitration
  b <= #proposers = |regs| - 1.  CONSEQUENCE: a TWO-proposer conflict record
  has Bl = 2 exactly like a delivery circuit and CANNOT exceed 4 at d = 2,
  despite carrying 3-register events.  Width past 4 needs 3+ PROPOSERS, not
  merely 3+ registers — W4b's necessary condition is not sufficient.

  W4c's PROOF, WRITTEN IN (round-1 MAJOR 3: the [THEOREM] tag was carried by a
  per-record census, i.e. by a measurement, and its printed reason — '`regs_of`
  places a version name in exactly one event's register set' — was at the wrong
  level, since `regs_of` is a function of ONE event while 'occurs once' is a
  property of the RECORD.  Four steps, each checked against the committed
  d42b1 source, whose lines are quoted below:
    (1) A version occupies a register ONLY where it is born.  `regs_of` returns
        {a} for p/n, {sender, receiver} for d, {a, ('mw',a,pk)} for m, and
        props u {vname(base, op[3], op[1])} for r.  A DELIVERY OF VERSION v
        THEREFORE CARRIES v IN ITS PAYLOAD op[3] AND DOES NOT OCCUPY v's
        REGISTER — which is exactly why the minted wire is dead.  Merge-created
        names (`mname`) never appear in any `regs_of` at all.
    (2) So a version register can recur only if TWO DISTINCT ARBITRATIONS MINT
        THE SAME `vname` — i.e. share the base, the winner key's value tuple,
        the winner key's author tuple, and the initiator.
    (3) Two such arbitrations share at least one PROPOSER REGISTER (the winner
        authors, and the initiator, are proposers of both).  In `event_poset`,
        once an event touches register r it becomes last[r] and every later
        event touching r inherits its whole past — so the two are CAUSALLY
        COMPARABLE.
    (4) The later one's `View` therefore contains the earlier arbitration, so
        base is in view.superseded; `arb_components_in_view` SKIPS components
        whose base is superseded, `admissible` finds no matching component and
        returns False.  THE SECOND ARBITRATION IS INADMISSIBLE.  QED.
      [committed d42b1, line 74] if k == 'd': return frozenset([op[1], op[2]])    <== regs_of(d) is {sender, receiver}, not the payload
      [committed d42b1, line 79] return frozenset(props | {vname(base, op[3], op[1])})    <== regs_of(r) is proposers u {the minted vname}
      [committed d42b1, line 88] pred[j] |= pred[last[r]] | {last[r]}    <== event_poset inherits the whole past through a register
      [committed d42b1, line 108] self.superseded.add(base)    <== an arbitration supersedes its base in the View
      [committed d42b1, line 236] if base in view.superseded: continue    <== arb_components_in_view SKIPS a superseded base
      [committed d42b1, line 324] if not match: return False, None    <== admissible(r) refuses when no component matches
  [PASS] A2(b0) [MAJOR 3, THE THEOREM TAG NOW CARRIES AN ARGUMENT] W4c's LOAD-BEARING STEP IS PROVED FROM THE COMMITTED LAYER, NOT MEASURED.  Every line the four-step proof leans on is quoted above and gated to occur VERBATIM in the committed d42b1_transport_exact.py: that a delivery's registers are {sender, receiver} and not the payload version; that an arbitration's registers are its proposers plus the minted name; that `event_poset` makes any two events sharing a register causally comparable; that an arbitration supersedes its base; that `arb_components_in_view` skips a superseded base; and that `admissible` refuses an arbitration with no matching component.  The per-record census below is kept as EVIDENCE beside the proof, not as its warrant  (source lines gated verbatim = 6 of 6)

  THE WIDTH LAW, CORRECTED (round-1 BLOCKER 1).  W4c's bound is NOT attained at
  2k as a law: `2k` is the value of the refined bound when every depth-1
  successor of an arbitration is a TWO-register event, i.e. a delivery, which
  is what the RING and GRID blueprints impose and nothing in the grammar
  forces.  THE REFINEMENT.  Every P-edge raises the height by at least 1, so a
  depth-2 direction is reached from e by a P-path of length 1 or 2, i.e.
       D_e(2)  SUBSET OF  succ(e)  UNION  { succ(y) : y in succ(e) },
  an EXACT containment, gated below at every event of every record.  When e
  has at least one successor at height h(e) + 1 — i.e. when no P-edge out of
  e skips a layer — the first term contributes nothing at depth 2 and the
  bound is the sharp |D_e(2)| <= SUM_{y in succ(e)} b(y) <= b(e) * Bl <= k * Bl,
  which is <= k^2 = W4c's own ceiling for a k-proposer arbitration.  A3b
  builds a record in which an arbitration's successors are ARBITRATIONS and
  SATURATES k^2 = 9.  The exceptions to the SHARP form are counted and
  CHARACTERISED below rather than hidden: they are exactly the events all of
  whose P-successors sit two or more layers above them (a height-skipping
  edge into a terminal arbitration at a record's end).
